    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Upper Cretaceous Cerro Toro Formation, consisting of the deep water clastic sediments of the Magallanes Foreland Basin, crops out in a wide area in Chilean Patagonia. The Formation consists of coarse and fine grained units of varying thickness.\r\n\r\nThe study area, located in the Parque Nacional Torres Del Paine, has been deformed by folding, with N-S trending fold axis, by N-S oriented thrust faults, and by an apparent conjugate system of N70°E and N80°W strike-slip faults, the former being right-lateral and the latter being left-lateral. Additionally, the area has frequent igneous intrusions.\r\n\r\nThree goals that have been identified from the recent field work are; 1) To better understand the relationship between thrust faulting and folding, including shortening amounts and the occurrence of faulting before during and after deposition, 2) To determine the genetic relationships between initial jointing and the larger scale faulting observed in the area, and 3) to understand the role of igneous dike intrusions along fault traces and in folds\r\n\r\nPreliminary data provide some insight into these questions; thrust fault occurrence appears to be controlled by lithology, as the majority of thrust faults are in fine grained material. Shearing along cross joint sets is the likely source of the larger faults in the study area. Igeneous dikes generally occur along strike-slip faults, which appear to be the main conduits for fluid and magma flow in the area.",
